# Which deployment this is. Drives Secure cookies, the JWT_SECRET requirement,
# and which Midtrans gateway is used.
#   production — the real site. Real money.
#   sandbox    — the public sandbox host. Strict like production, fake money.
#   local      — a developer laptop. Relaxed cookies, insecure JWT fallback.
APP_ENV=local

# Per-environment - each deployment (prod, sandbox, local) needs its own
# database, secrets, and redirect URI. Never share these values across
# environments.
#
# DB_DSN: full libpq/pgx connection string. The host part is written from the
# BACKEND's point of view, not yours: under docker compose the database lives
# on the host machine, not in the stack, so containers reach it through
# host.docker.internal. Running a cmd/ tool straight from Windows instead of
# inside a container? Swap that for localhost or nothing resolves.
DB_DSN=postgres://postgres:postgres@host.docker.internal:5432/crowdflow?sslmode=disable

# Signs user access + refresh tokens (HS256). Required whenever APP_ENV is not
# local — main.go fatals on boot rather than start with a guessable key. On
# local only, it falls back to the hardcoded "crowdflow_dev_jwt_secret", which
# is public knowledge and must never reach a deployed host. Rotating it logs
# every user out. Generate with: openssl rand -base64 48
JWT_SECRET=

# Signs ticketman (scanner staff) sessions. Deliberately a SEPARATE secret from
# JWT_SECRET above — aud is not checked anywhere the platform's own auth
# middleware runs, so a ticketman token signed with the platform key would be
# accepted by ordinary user routes. A distinct key makes that impossible by
# construction. Never reuse JWT_SECRET's value here.
TICKETMAN_JWT_SECRET=

# Where Google sends the browser back after consent. Must match an Authorized
# redirect URI registered in the Google Cloud console CHARACTER FOR CHARACTER —
# scheme, host, port and path — or the consent screen errors before your code
# runs. Path is fixed by the router: /api/v1/auth/google/callback.
# For local dev use http://localhost/api/v1/auth/google/callback through nginx
# on port 80 — pointing it at :3000 or :8080 breaks the oauth_state cookie,
# because the cookie is set on one origin and read back on another.
# Defaults to the localhost form when empty.
GOOGLE_REDIRECT_URI=

# Base URL of the deployed frontend, no trailing slash. Two consumers depend
# on it. E-ticket emails (internal/payment's Midtrans webhook dispatch) build
# every booking link as {FRONTEND_URL}/booking/<order_uuid> and
# .../t/<ticket_uuid> — with no PDF or QR-image fallback (plan decision 24)
# that link is the only way a buyer reaches their ticket, so a wrong value
# reaches a real customer as a dead link. The Google OAuth redirect and
# password-reset emails also send the browser here, and this value is trusted
# outright. Required outside local dev — main.go refuses to boot without it
# unless APP_ENV=local.
FRONTEND_URL=http://localhost:3000

# Comma-separated origins accepted only when FRONTEND_URL is unset. The OAuth
# redirect and reset-email flows then validate the request's own Origin/Host
# against this list instead of trusting them: nginx forwards any Host header
# verbatim, so an unvalidated fallback there is an open account-takeover
# vector. Not needed on a developer laptop (APP_ENV=local) — a hardcoded
# localhost allowlist covers that case.
ALLOWED_FRONTEND_ORIGINS=

# AES-256 key for encrypting attendee NIK at rest (internal/nik). Base64,
# must decode to exactly 32 bytes. Generate with:
#   openssl rand -base64 32
# Never share this across environments — a leaked sandbox key must not
# decrypt production NIK data.
NIK_ENC_KEY=

# Google OAuth client credentials, from the same Cloud console project that
# owns GOOGLE_REDIRECT_URI above. The secret is a real credential — it belongs
# in the host's .env, never in a committed file or a NEXT_PUBLIC_ var.
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID has a hardcoded fallback in main.go for local convenience;
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET has none, so "Sign in with Google" simply fails at the
# token exchange if it is blank.
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=

# Object storage (internal/storage). S3-compatible, so the same six values
# point at MinIO locally and Cloudflare R2 on a deployed host — swapping
# providers is an env change, not a code change.
#
# Two buckets, deliberately: PUBLIC holds anything served straight to a
# browser (event covers, avatars); PRIVATE holds identity documents (KTP,
# NPWP, NIB) that are only ever reachable through a short-lived presigned URL
# the backend mints after an auth check.
#
# On R2, S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID / S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY come from an API token scoped
# to these buckets. The token VALUE that Cloudflare shows you goes nowhere —
# only the Access Key ID and Secret do. A scoped token cannot ListBuckets, so
# a failure there is normal and is not evidence the credential is broken.
#
# S3_REGION defaults to "auto" (correct for R2) when empty.
S3_ENDPOINT=
S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
S3_PUBLIC_BUCKET_NAME=
S3_PRIVATE_BUCKET_NAME=
S3_REGION=

# Public read URL for the PUBLIC bucket, used to turn a stored object key into
# a browsable link. The R2 public bucket URL or a custom domain in front of it.
# WARNING: when this is empty the code does not error — it silently falls back
# to http://localhost:9000/crowdflow-public, so a deployed host with this unset
# serves every image as a localhost link that only works on the server itself.
S3_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=http://localhost:9000/crowdflow-public

# Midtrans — the keys live in the per-environment example files, so a sandbox
# host never has a production key on disk. Copy from whichever applies:
#   .env.sandbox.example     (from dashboard.sandbox.midtrans.com)
#   .env.production.example  (from dashboard.midtrans.com)
#
# Do not judge a key by its prefix: SB- means sandbox, but its ABSENCE proves
# nothing — sandbox keys without the prefix exist. To settle which gateway a key
# belongs to, ask Midtrans:
#   curl -u "$MIDTRANS_SERVER_KEY:" \
#     https://api.sandbox.midtrans.com/v2/any-unknown-order-id/status
# 404 "Transaction doesn't exist" = valid for that gateway. 401 = not.
MIDTRANS_SERVER_KEY=

# Resend Mail Service — transactional email: signup OTP, password reset, and
# the e-ticket booking link sent from the Midtrans webhook.
#
# RESEND_API_KEY empty is NOT an error and NOT a no-op you will notice: every
# send is swallowed and logged as "[MAIL MOCK] Sending email to: ..." instead.
# Fine locally, silent total mail loss on a deployed host.
#
# RESEND_FROM_EMAIL must be an address at a domain VERIFIED in the Resend
# dashboard (SPF/DKIM DNS records published and green). The default below,
# onboarding@resend.dev, is Resend's shared sandbox sender: it only ever
# delivers to the Resend account owner's own address and is silently dropped
# for everyone else. Since the booking link is the only route a buyer has to
# their ticket, shipping with this default means real buyers receive nothing.
RESEND_API_KEY=
RESEND_FROM_EMAIL=CrowdFlow <onboarding@resend.dev>

# Cloudflare Turnstile — the SECRET half of the widget pair; the site key is
# NEXT_PUBLIC_TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY in the frontend build. Left empty, the backend
# falls back to Cloudflare's always-passes test secret, so the CAPTCHA is
# decorative. Set the real secret on any host reachable from the internet.
TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY=
